Note = {Workshop on Perception and Action, Giessen, GERMANY, AUG, 2005},
Abstract = {This study shows that in a novel task-bimanual haptic
   tracking-neurologically normal human adults can move their 2 hands
   independently for extended periods of time with little or no training.
   Participants lightly touched buttons whose positions were moved either
   quasi-randomly in the horizontal plane by 1 or 2 human drivers
   (Experiment 1), in circle and square patterns in the vertical plane by 2
   human drivers (Experiment 2), or at different frequencies in the
   horizontal plane by 2 human drivers (Experiment 3). Bimanual contact was
   maintained equally well in all conditions even though in Experiment 1
   the left- and right-hand motions were uncorrelated (in the 2-driver
   condition), in Experiment 2 the left- and right-hand motions were
   spatially incongruous when circles and squares were tracked at the same
   time, and in Experiment 3 the left- and right-hand motions maintained
   different frequency ratios. Because haptic tracking has revealed that
   humans can in fact move their 2 hands independently, it may have
   potential as a new behavioral tool for revealing other perceptual-motor
   capabilities.},
