   02-05, 2004},
Abstract = {Scene recognition has been found to be sensitive to the orientation of
   the scene with respect to the stationary observer. Recent studies have
   shown, however, that observer movement can compensate for changes in
   visual scene orientation, through a process of spatial updating. Here we
   investigated whether spatial updating in scene recognition is affected
   by the encoding or learning modality by examining whether observer
   movement can also compensate for orientation changes in haptic scene
   recognition. In experiment 1, we replicated previously reported effects
   of observer movement on visual scene recognition. In experiment 2, we
   used the same apparatus as in experiment 1 but here participants were
   required to learn and recognize the scenes using touch alone. We found a
   cost in recognition performance with changes in scene orientation
   relative to the stationary observer. However, when participants could
   move around the scene to recognize the new orientation, then this cost
   in recognition performance disappeared. Thus, we found that spatial
   updating applies to recognition in both the visual and haptic
   modalities, both of which intrinsically encode the spatial properties of
   a scene.},
