Organization = {Hong Kong Polytechn Univ, Fac Construct \& Land Use; Inst Urban
   Environm; Road King Infrastructure Ltd; Sun Hung Kai Properties; Chun Wo
   Dev Holdings Ltd; K C Wong Educ Fdn; Environm \& Conservat Fund; Gammon;
   Paul Y; Architecture Design \& Res Grp Ltd (AD+RG); China State
   Construct (CSCEC); Sino Grp; Vantage Int (Holdings) Ltd; ASCE Hong Kong
   Sect; ASHRAE Hong Kong Chapter; CIBSE; Construct Ind Council (CIC);
   Chartered Inst Bldg (CIOB); Council Sustainable Dev; Emerald; Hong Kong
   Inst Architects; Hong Kong Institut Engineers; Hong Kong Inst Surveyors;
   HKSTS; IUE; JSCE; RICS; Urban Planning Soc China; China Civil Engn Soc},
Abstract = {In China, more and more construction and demolition (C\&D) wastes are
   produced now. The wastes will not only occupy plenty of land, but also
   bring a series of serious problem to the environment. Though concrete
   demolition waste has been proved to be an excellent source of coarse
   aggregates for new concrete production, the fine fraction of the C\&D
   wastes has not been the subject of thorough similar studies. This paper
   concerns the use of recycled fine concrete and clay brick aggregates to
   respectively globally replace natural fine aggregates (sand) in the
   production of mixed mortar. To evaluate the viability of this process,
   an experimental campaign was implemented in order to monitor the
   fundamental properties of such mortar. The fundamental properties such
   as the compressive strength and the shrinkage ratio of mixed mortar made
   of the recycled fine aggregates are tested and discussed in detail. The
   experimental results indicated that it is viable to produce mixed mortar
   using recycled fine concrete and clay brick aggregates, the compressive
   strength of mixed mortar made of recycled fine aggregates is lower than
   that of mixed mortar made of natural fine aggregates and the shrinkage
   ratio of mixed mortar made of recycled fine aggregates is larger, cured
   in natural environment.},
