Year = {2023},
Pages = {1015-1026},
Note = {2023 World Environmental and Water Resources Congress, Henderson, NV,
   MAY 21-24, 2023},
Abstract = {Lacking required information is a challenge for the analysis of water
   distribution networks (WDNs). This missing data often encompasses
   network information such as the location and attributes of pipes.
   Therefore, an automatic reconstruction approach is needed to rebuild the
   missing data of WDNs with limited available information. To address this
   issue, a novel data reconstruction method is developed in this work
   based on complex network theory and using graph metrics. As a showcase
   with a complete data set, scenarios with missing diameter information
   are systematically created and successively reconstructed based on graph
   characteristics of available diameters and neighboring pipes. To
   validate our approach, a WDN is used as the case study, where
   information about pipe diameters is gradually deleted to increase the
   data gap. Afterward, the structural (i.e., diameter) and hydraulic
   (i.e., pressure) features of the reconstructed networks are compared
   with the complete data set of that WDN. The results show that a
   significant part of unknown diameters can be successfully reconstructed
   with high accuracy until the 50\% data gap. Our framework can
   significantly assist water companies/urban planners who commonly face
   incomplete data within their models.},
